+# Stock behaviour research
+
+## Request paths and data model
+
+- Stock is stored in `ProductAvailability.productQuantity`, attached to a `Product` (and, for variants, potentially to a `ProductVariant`). The v2 product response is built by `ProductFacadeV2Impl` -> `ReadableProductMapper`/`ReadableProductPopulator`; the populator copies an availability quantity to `ReadableProduct.quantity` and only sets `canBePurchased` when quantity is positive and the product is marked available.
+- Cart creation/addition enters `ShoppingCartFacadeImpl.addToCart` and `readableShoppingCart`, then `createCartItem`. Quantity changes enter `modifyCart`; batch changes enter `modifyCartMulti` -> `createCartItems`. The facade saves the JPA cart after changing line-item entities.
+- Both checkout endpoints are in `OrderApi`: `/cart/{code}/checkout` and `/auth/cart/{code}/checkout`. They call `OrderFacadeImpl.processOrder`, which copies cart items into order products and delegates persistence/payment to `OrderServiceImpl.processOrder`.
+- Merchant order status updates enter `OrderApi.updateOrderStatus`, load the order, and call `OrderFacadeImpl.updateOrderStatus`, which currently only changes status/history.
+
+## Causes found
+
+1. `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/services/order/OrderServiceImpl.java` decrements inventory using `productService.getById(orderProduct.getId())`. `OrderProduct.id` is the order-line primary key, not the catalog product id. This explains another product being decremented or no product being found. The code then loops all product availabilities, subtracts the order quantity, and calls `productService.update`.
+2. The same method logs an inventory mismatch when quantity is insufficient but does not throw. It also creates/saves the order before its inventory loop. This violates all-or-nothing checkout and can create negative/incorrect stock.
+3. `ShoppingCartFacadeImpl.createCartItem` and `createCartItems` reject only null/zero availability. They do not compare requested quantity with stock. `readableShoppingCart` increments an existing line before a final quantity check, and `modifyCart` mutates existing line items before saving. `modifyCartMulti` builds all incoming items and then mutates the persisted set in a loop; there is no pre-validation or rollback, so a later invalid change can leave earlier in-memory/database changes.
+4. The cart API wraps ordinary failures in `ServiceRuntimeException`, whose handler returns HTTP 400. There is no dedicated conflict exception/handler, so stock conflicts cannot reliably produce HTTP 409.
+5. The authenticated checkout endpoint catches every exception, calls `sendError(503, ...)`, and returns null. The anonymous endpoint wraps failures in `ServiceRuntimeException`; neither maps inventory shortage to 409.
+6. `OrderFacadeImpl.updateOrderStatus` returns only for the same status and otherwise always changes status/history. It has no special handling for `CANCELED`, no inventory restoration, and no prevention of transitions away from an already canceled order. The status endpoint uses `OrderStatus.valueOf` and delegates directly.
+7. The readable product populator selects an arbitrary availability from a set and sets `canBePurchased` only inside the positive-quantity branch, but the initial readable product availability state can remain true. The response must explicitly make both `available` and `canBePurchased` false when effective stock is zero, while preserving merchant-unavailable products as unavailable even after restocking.
+
+## Other affected behaviour
+
+- `OrderProductPopulator` correctly snapshots the cart line SKU and quantity into the order line, so SKU is the stable identity to use for inventory adjustments.
+- Product availability is a set and may include regional/variant rows. Existing checkout/cart code generally checks every product availability, while the product response uses one selected row. The implementation needs one consistent effective-stock selection for the ordinary product SKU and must avoid changing unrelated products.
+- Cart operations need to validate the proposed complete cart before mutating or persisting it. Decrease/removal (quantity zero) must remain allowed even if current stock is zero.
+- Inventory changes and order/status persistence need transaction boundaries so a rejected checkout/status change leaves no partial stock/order state. Existing payment/order processing happens in the core order service, so the stock check must occur before creating the order; stock deduction must be tied to the successful order transaction.
+
+## Files expected to change
+
+- `sm-core/.../OrderServiceImpl.java` and likely its service interface/model support for atomic stock adjustment and restoration.
+- `sm-shop/.../ShoppingCartFacadeImpl.java` for proposed-cart quantity validation and conflict propagation.
+- `sm-shop/.../OrderApi.java` / `OrderFacadeImpl.java` for 409 checkout/status behaviour and cancellation idempotence/terminal-state rules.
+- `sm-shop/.../ReadableProductPopulator.java` (and possibly the v2 mapper) for explicit zero-stock availability flags.
+- `sm-shop/.../RestErrorHandler.java` or a dedicated conflict exception for HTTP 409.
+- Tests in the cart/order/product integration suites should cover multi-product identity, atomic failures, cart updates, cancellation, and v2 availability.
